00:03:03.610 --> 00:03:06.220
When I think about a
project that's being done,

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:08.780
there are just three components
I usually think about.

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:11.410
One is the developer--
that's you--

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:13.900
who is usually writing the code.

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:17.530
And that's the code, in one of
the corners of the triangle.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:19.460
But that code doesn't
just run in a vacuum,

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:21.647
but rather, it runs on some
kind of infrastructure.

00:03:21.647 --> 00:03:23.230
There's some environment
that supports

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:25.300
the code that can be run.

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:27.460
And that environment,
in turn, has been set up

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:29.507
by you, again, the developer.

00:03:29.507 --> 00:03:30.840
And so that's the triangle here.

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:34.120
Now, if you are a single
developer on a one-person

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:36.970
team-- that's yourself--
working on a single project,

00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:40.420
a single code base, in an
always-consistent environment--

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:41.590
say, your laptop--

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:43.300
then, probably, you
don't need Docker,

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:45.240
to be completely
honest with you.

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:48.730
But any moment, if any
of these three corners,

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:52.570
I guess, of the
triangle changes,

00:03:52.570 --> 00:03:54.430
Docker might help
you in some ways.

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:57.970
That's the pitch I have tonight.

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:01.620
So you can imagine, how can
the developer corner change?

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:03.370
Well, you might need
to share your project

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:04.650
with someone else on the team.

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:05.890
If I'm working on a
multiple-person team,

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:08.080
maybe you need to hand
it off to your engineer

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:10.948
if you are in the role
of a data scientist.

00:04:10.948 --> 00:04:13.240
Or the code-- you might be
working on multiple projects

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:13.948
at the same time.

00:04:13.948 --> 00:04:16.180
You might be maintaining
and, in fact, improving

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:19.990
more than one
machine-learning-based services

00:04:19.990 --> 00:04:23.590
in your production
environment at the same time.

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:26.440
Environment-- you might be doing
experimentation on your laptop

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:30.070
in, maybe, notebooks or some
other kind of configuration,

00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:32.650
and you do want to move
it to the cloud somewhere

00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:36.310
for serving, or for
larger-scale training.

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:40.750
And so that is when you have
a change in the environment

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:42.070
angle of the triangle.

00:04:42.070 --> 00:04:44.410
And so whenever any
of these changes,

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:48.550
you might be in a situation
of dependency inconsistency.

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:50.320
And so that's when
Docker might help you.

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:51.965
But I do want to
point out that Docker

00:04:51.965 --> 00:04:54.340
isn't the only solution for
any of these problems, right?

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:55.965
There are other ways
to deal with this.

00:04:55.965 --> 00:04:59.840
For example, normally, for
users who do need to work on--

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:03.550
for developers who need to
work on multiple projects

00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:06.680
in the same environment and be
local on your local machine,

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:09.090
usually, people will go
with a virtual environment--

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:10.240
say in Python.

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:12.190
And most programming
languages nowadays

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:16.410
have some form of dependency
management tools for you.

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:17.680
So that is sort of handled.

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:21.160
And environment as well--
there are many different ways

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:23.080
to migrate from one
environment to another

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:26.990
without all kind of headaches
that I hope to tell you

00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:29.830
a little bit about tonight.

00:05:29.830 --> 00:05:31.840
But Docker happens
to be a sufficiently

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:33.730
flexible and powerful
tool that handles

00:05:33.730 --> 00:05:35.540
all this in a consistent way.

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:39.100
And so the kind
of good habit I'm

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:41.650
hoping to promote
in this session

00:05:41.650 --> 00:05:44.890
is that, with a fairly
small amount of overhead,

00:05:44.890 --> 00:05:47.560
when you set up a project and
when you work on a project,

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:51.400
you'll be able to remove some
of these obstacles later on.

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:53.680
If you foresee that,
there'll be a point in time

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:55.910
when I need to hand this
project off to someone else,

00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:57.970
or even just start
collaborating with someone else,

00:05:57.970 --> 00:05:59.345
or if you see that
at some point,

00:05:59.345 --> 00:06:02.050
you want to smoothly switch
between different projects

00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:04.750
or move to different
environments,

00:06:04.750 --> 00:06:09.010
you might consider starting
out with a good habit.

00:06:09.010 --> 00:06:13.570
And this is really general about
software development projects

00:06:13.570 --> 00:06:17.410
and not specific about
machine learning workload.

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:20.490
But honestly, a lot of times,
machine learning workloads

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:23.870
have this hidden dependency that
people don't talk enough about,

00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:25.220
in my opinion.

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:30.130
So in my own example,
in a previous team,

00:06:30.130 --> 00:06:33.010
I had worked on machine
learning projects back-to-back.

00:06:33.010 --> 00:06:34.660
Back then, we used scikit-learn.

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:36.250
And I deployed one
model-- so great.

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:38.980
And then, when I moved on to
the next project, I thought,

00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:42.220
I will be upgrading my
scikit-learn version,

00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:44.110
and then to use a
new feature which

00:06:44.110 --> 00:06:46.510
I needed for the next project.

00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:47.860
So it saves me a lot of time.

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:49.582
Only, when I deployed
that, I realized

00:06:49.582 --> 00:06:51.040
I was breaking the
previous project

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:52.660
because the previous
project could not

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:54.730
be loaded into a newer
version of scikit-learn.

00:06:54.730 --> 00:06:55.960
And I don't know if this
has happened to you.

00:06:55.960 --> 00:06:58.440
For TensorFlow, I had the
exact same issue as well.

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:01.340
I had old checkpoints trained
with the older version

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:01.840
TensorFlow.

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:04.300
It just does not load for me
anymore with a newer version.

00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:06.717
And so you can imagine, if
you're a data scientist working

00:07:06.717 --> 00:07:09.560
in this situation, you might
have deployed a whole model

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:12.900
somewhere already, and the
requirement has changed,

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:15.667
or the need has arisen so that
you need to improve the model.

00:07:15.667 --> 00:07:18.000
You would like to load the
old checkpoint without having

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:19.297
to redo all the experiments.

00:07:19.297 --> 00:07:20.880
It doesn't work
anymore because you've

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:24.262
uploaded-- so you have updated
your TensorFlow version.

00:07:24.262 --> 00:07:26.720
So that can be very subtle,
because these things are really

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:27.510
hard to pin down.

00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:31.310
A lot of times, people will
go with [? PEEP ?] requirement

00:07:31.310 --> 00:07:34.790
files and say, this is a list
of key packages that's needed.

00:07:34.790 --> 00:07:38.720
And that's what we did
before, at the previous team.

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:42.410
Only when we realized that we
were using a single requirement

00:07:42.410 --> 00:07:46.670
file for that a single
one monolithic code base,

00:07:46.670 --> 00:07:49.170
and that's causing
problems for everyone who's

00:07:49.170 --> 00:07:51.473
using the same code base,
because I'll be saying,

00:07:51.473 --> 00:07:54.140
hey, guys, I need to update this
particular scikit-learn version

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:56.260
to a fancier version
because I needed it,

00:07:56.260 --> 00:07:59.280
and that breaks anyone else's
work because they can't use it.

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:00.773
And that particular
case happened

00:08:00.773 --> 00:08:03.190
because scikit-learn was making
a very significant change.

00:08:03.190 --> 00:08:09.128
They moved a bunch of code from
pipe down into Java back-end.

00:08:09.128 --> 00:08:10.670
And so that caused
a whole lot issues

00:08:10.670 --> 00:08:13.790
because some interfaces
suddenly disappeared.

00:08:13.790 --> 00:08:16.440
We do hope that similar
things will not happen to you,

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:19.755
but if it does, it'll
be a lot of headache.

00:08:19.755 --> 00:08:21.380
And the end result
was something like I

00:08:21.380 --> 00:08:25.130
had to spend a whole
month trying to figure out

00:08:25.130 --> 00:08:27.260
what's the update path
for the whole code base

00:08:27.260 --> 00:08:29.090
so that no project
will break, I can still

00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:31.460
use a new feature I want,
old models can still

00:08:31.460 --> 00:08:34.159
be deployed to production.

00:08:34.159 --> 00:08:34.910
A lot of problems.

00:08:34.910 --> 00:08:37.142
And so these are the
benefits of Docker,

00:08:37.142 --> 00:08:39.350
exactly corresponding to
those three kinds of changes

00:08:39.350 --> 00:08:40.370
I was talking about.

00:08:40.370 --> 00:08:43.220
For consistency-- if
you want to run code

00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:46.730
in the exact same environment,
Docker helps you do that.

00:08:46.730 --> 00:08:49.860
For isolation-- if you want to
work between different projects

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:51.860
with very quick switch,
yes, virtual environment

00:08:51.860 --> 00:08:54.920
is super fast too in terms
of switching projects.

00:08:54.920 --> 00:08:57.680
Docker can do that as well.

00:08:57.680 --> 00:09:01.650
Collaboration is really
the bigger piece here.

00:09:01.650 --> 00:09:03.800
And by "collaboration,"
I mean a little bit more

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:05.355
than code-level collaboration.

00:09:05.355 --> 00:09:06.980
A lot of times, you
do need to hand off

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:11.000
a whole deployable project,
a bundle of some sort,

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:15.860
to your engineer, and then
you'll deploy that to somewhere

00:09:15.860 --> 00:09:17.240
on your infrastructure.

00:09:17.240 --> 00:09:19.070
And Docker makes
this much, much more

00:09:19.070 --> 00:09:23.510
seamless because the
environment that is, first

00:09:23.510 --> 00:09:27.145
of all, declared in an
understandable way-- that's

00:09:27.145 --> 00:09:32.570
really important-- and
making the results much more

00:09:32.570 --> 00:09:34.200
reproducible.

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:36.380
So I want to--

00:09:36.380 --> 00:09:38.660
just for the benefit
of those who may not

00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:40.560
have been using
Docker for a while,

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:43.010
I have a little bit of a
mini cheat sheet here showing

00:09:43.010 --> 00:09:44.960
some of the commands
I'll be using today

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:50.240
for I think, in a
few minutes now,

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:52.760
I will switch into more,
like, a light demo mode

00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:55.705
and show you one
particular workflow.

00:09:55.705 --> 00:09:57.830
So illustrating all these
points I was making here.

00:09:57.830 --> 00:09:59.478
So the base is Docker.

00:09:59.478 --> 00:10:01.520
There's these two things
that are often confused.

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:03.600
There's a Docker image
and a Docker container.

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:04.520
A Docker image is
just-- you should

00:10:04.520 --> 00:10:06.062
think of that as
software bundle that

00:10:06.062 --> 00:10:09.290
comes with the dependencies
already installed in there.

00:10:09.290 --> 00:10:11.810
And the container is a
running instance of that.

00:10:11.810 --> 00:10:14.300
So the same image could
be run multiple times.

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:17.005
So you could have one
image being run five times

00:10:17.005 --> 00:10:18.380
and get five
different containers

00:10:18.380 --> 00:10:20.600
all running the same image.

00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:23.450
So think of them
as five processes

00:10:23.450 --> 00:10:27.320
running the same code, and so
these are some basic commands.

00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:30.120
You can pull an image that
people have published.

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:33.950
For example, here I'm
showing a standard way

00:10:33.950 --> 00:10:37.590
to identify a Docker image.

00:10:37.590 --> 00:10:41.106
So GCR stands for
Google Cloud Registry.

00:10:41.106 --> 00:10:45.410
It holds Docker images for
you, so you can pull it

00:10:45.410 --> 00:10:49.190
if you will have built and
published Docker images,

00:10:49.190 --> 00:10:51.680
and you can--

00:10:51.680 --> 00:10:54.320
docker space images basically
lists all the images

00:10:54.320 --> 00:10:57.390
you have pulled
from repositories.

00:10:57.390 --> 00:11:00.410
rmi stands for Remove Image.

00:11:00.410 --> 00:11:01.400
That's really handy.

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:03.567
There'll be a time when you
have way too many Docker

00:11:03.567 --> 00:11:07.310
images on your laptop, and
you may want to remove them.

00:11:07.310 --> 00:11:09.560
And to run them, you
basically use the same syntax.

00:11:09.560 --> 00:11:13.068
Also, by the way, here, whenever
I mentioned REPOSITORY:TAG,

00:11:13.068 --> 00:11:14.360
that's the same thing up there.

00:11:14.360 --> 00:11:18.380
So that whole thing, gcr.io,
everything after colon is,

00:11:18.380 --> 00:11:22.040
in Docker language,
called the repository.

00:11:22.040 --> 00:11:24.100
And after the colon is the tag.

00:11:24.100 --> 00:11:28.760
And so you might be
pulling different things,

00:11:28.760 --> 00:11:30.370
like different versions.

00:11:30.370 --> 00:11:33.030
And if you have a
container that's running,

00:11:33.030 --> 00:11:33.720
you can kill it.

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:36.173
Again, Docker container is--

00:11:36.173 --> 00:11:38.340
you should think of that
as the running process that

00:11:38.340 --> 00:11:43.820
runs a specific piece of
binary, perhaps, or executable.

00:11:43.820 --> 00:11:46.490
We should think of it that way.

00:11:46.490 --> 00:11:49.430
Questions so far?

00:11:49.430 --> 00:11:50.000
Good?

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:50.630
OK.

00:11:50.630 --> 00:11:51.860
A little bit more.

00:11:51.860 --> 00:11:54.977
And Docker file is
really the declaration

00:11:54.977 --> 00:11:56.060
of how to build the image.

00:11:56.060 --> 00:11:57.477
We're going to go
through a really

00:11:57.477 --> 00:11:59.370
baby example of that today.

00:11:59.370 --> 00:12:03.360
And then, these next two
things are really interesting.

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:05.260
I'll make some more
points in a minute.

00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:06.390
Like a mount value--

00:12:06.390 --> 00:12:09.320
so a Docker container
is a process

00:12:09.320 --> 00:12:12.110
that's running on a host
machine, maybe on my laptop.

00:12:12.110 --> 00:12:13.610
So what happens is
that, on the base

00:12:13.610 --> 00:12:15.770
here, that my laptop has
its own operating system,

00:12:15.770 --> 00:12:18.062
and on top of that, it could
be running multiple Docker

00:12:18.062 --> 00:12:19.530
containers at the same time.

00:12:19.530 --> 00:12:22.610
So those are called Docker
containers processes

00:12:22.610 --> 00:12:24.373
running on top of the host.

00:12:24.373 --> 00:12:26.290
And so there's the way
to communicate the host

00:12:26.290 --> 00:12:28.132
and the container themselves.

00:12:28.132 --> 00:12:29.340
And these are two basic ways.

00:12:29.340 --> 00:12:30.970
One is by mounting
a volume if you

00:12:30.970 --> 00:12:34.662
want to share part of your
host disk with a process--

00:12:34.662 --> 00:12:35.870
which is extremely important.

00:12:35.870 --> 00:12:37.580
I'll get to it in a minute.

00:12:37.580 --> 00:12:40.070
And another way is
to forward a port.

00:12:40.070 --> 00:12:42.860
So maybe your container
is running a service,

00:12:42.860 --> 00:12:46.550
and by exposing some kind
of port on the container,

00:12:46.550 --> 00:12:48.710
you do want the host to
be able to access that

00:12:48.710 --> 00:12:51.020
from outside of the container.

00:12:51.020 --> 00:12:52.520
And that's how the
way you will just

00:12:52.520 --> 00:12:56.790
forward a host, port number,
colon, a container port number.

00:12:56.790 --> 00:12:58.760
So it's the same syntax--

00:12:58.760 --> 00:13:00.680
host container ordering.

00:13:06.770 --> 00:13:10.150
With all the other nice things
I could say about Docker,

00:13:10.150 --> 00:13:11.660
there are a few gotchas.

00:13:11.660 --> 00:13:13.460
And so one of the most
important things--

00:13:13.460 --> 00:13:14.850
I cannot emphasize this enough.

00:13:14.850 --> 00:13:15.770
I tried.

00:13:15.770 --> 00:13:20.900
I have all capital, bold
italic font, and in highlight.

00:13:20.900 --> 00:13:23.150
Everything you're doing in
a container will be gone.

00:13:23.150 --> 00:13:24.150
Again, will be gone.

00:13:24.150 --> 00:13:26.960
I've seen this happening
to new Docker users

00:13:26.960 --> 00:13:29.130
and experienced users as well.

00:13:29.130 --> 00:13:31.610
They go into Docker,
they feel so at home,

00:13:31.610 --> 00:13:33.470
and they run all kinds
of downloaded stuff,

00:13:33.470 --> 00:13:37.860
install things, and run code,
they save a model locally.

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:39.610
And then, they exit the process.

00:13:39.610 --> 00:13:41.220
All that is gone.

00:13:41.220 --> 00:13:44.060
So the interesting thing is that
a Docker container feels just

00:13:44.060 --> 00:13:45.080
like a shell, right?

00:13:45.080 --> 00:13:46.030
You can connect to it.

00:13:46.030 --> 00:13:47.780
It's a process that's
a whole environment,

00:13:47.780 --> 00:13:49.322
It feels like a new
computer, almost.

00:13:49.322 --> 00:13:50.863
You can do all kinds
of things in it.

00:13:50.863 --> 00:13:52.430
It even has a file
system in there--

00:13:52.430 --> 00:13:56.560
except that file system
is not persistent.

00:13:56.560 --> 00:14:01.410
The moment you exit the
container, everything is gone--

00:14:01.410 --> 00:14:04.260
unless you were doing things
carefully and connected

00:14:04.260 --> 00:14:06.520
the running container with
a host in the first place.

00:14:06.520 --> 00:14:09.660
Now, everything you were writing
into the disk in a container

00:14:09.660 --> 00:14:11.790
does get returned
to your host disk.

00:14:11.790 --> 00:14:13.210
That way, it'll not be gone.

00:14:13.210 --> 00:14:16.740
And so that's really one
of the biggest gotchas

00:14:16.740 --> 00:14:21.100
I have wasted hours to recover
work just because of this

00:14:21.100 --> 00:14:24.533
until I learned
how to mount disks.

00:14:24.533 --> 00:14:25.450
But think about this--

00:14:25.450 --> 00:14:26.950
there's a downside
of mounted disks,

00:14:26.950 --> 00:14:28.710
because it means
your package has

00:14:28.710 --> 00:14:33.165
some additional extra
dependencies there.

00:14:33.165 --> 00:14:35.790
So you are mounting a disk, and
you download things, write code

00:14:35.790 --> 00:14:38.500
happily, but all that code is no
longer in the Docker container.

00:14:38.500 --> 00:14:41.460
So that means this Docker
container or image thing cannot

00:14:41.460 --> 00:14:44.040
be handed off easily anymore
because now it has to carry

00:14:44.040 --> 00:14:46.530
this additional baggage
of whatever code, or data,

00:14:46.530 --> 00:14:49.020
or model that you have
created on the side.

00:14:49.020 --> 00:14:51.630
So that's some trade-off
you need to think about.

00:14:51.630 --> 00:14:54.900
Normally, I would
recommend doing things

00:14:54.900 --> 00:14:57.708
with some of the cloud storage
on day one, if you can.

00:14:57.708 --> 00:14:59.250
If you foresee that
eventually that's

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:01.375
going to be your need
anyway, start writings things

00:15:01.375 --> 00:15:03.660
to a cloud somewhere.

00:15:03.660 --> 00:15:08.025
That way, you and your
team can easily share that.

00:15:08.025 --> 00:15:09.400
Another thing
that's also gone is

00:15:09.400 --> 00:15:13.420
that you connect into your
Docker instance, and then

00:15:13.420 --> 00:15:16.280
a process, and then
you install things.

00:15:16.280 --> 00:15:17.200
It will also be gone.

00:15:17.200 --> 00:15:19.140
And then you come around, and
your code, again, somehow it

00:15:19.140 --> 00:15:19.930
doesn't work anymore.

00:15:19.930 --> 00:15:21.805
It's complaining about
some packages missing.

00:15:21.805 --> 00:15:24.310
You say, wait a second,
I've installed that before--

00:15:24.310 --> 00:15:27.340
only, you have
exited the process.

00:15:27.340 --> 00:15:30.160
And so here, Docker
provides a handy way for you

00:15:30.160 --> 00:15:32.830
to sort of remember to
keep a note for that,

00:15:32.830 --> 00:15:34.610
is through the mechanism
of a Docker file.

00:15:34.610 --> 00:15:36.610
So every when you are in
a container and install

00:15:36.610 --> 00:15:39.420
something, make sure you record
that in your Docker file.

00:15:39.420 --> 00:15:41.920
That way, you'll never be lost,
and then everyone-- anyone--

00:15:41.920 --> 00:15:44.208
can come and reproduce that.

00:15:44.208 --> 00:15:46.500
Some other gotchas that we'll
not really get into today

00:15:46.500 --> 00:15:48.000
is networking.

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:48.970
It's a little tricky.

00:15:48.970 --> 00:15:50.970
That has to do with the
port forwarding business

00:15:50.970 --> 00:15:53.490
we mentioned before,
because Docker's containers

00:15:53.490 --> 00:15:56.190
are designed to be--

00:15:56.190 --> 00:15:58.390
one of the goals is
to be really secure.

00:15:58.390 --> 00:15:59.910
So we don't really
want containers

00:15:59.910 --> 00:16:01.327
to be talking to
the outside world

00:16:01.327 --> 00:16:04.135
very easily, for good reasons.

00:16:04.135 --> 00:16:05.760
And so sometimes,
people have struggled

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:08.160
with networking a little bit.

00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:09.810
If you are in a
situation where you

00:16:09.810 --> 00:16:12.380
do have to manage a
large number, even just,

00:16:12.380 --> 00:16:14.700
in any case, the
multiple containers that

00:16:14.700 --> 00:16:17.600
need to talk to each other or
the outside world and the host,

00:16:17.600 --> 00:16:19.427
that becomes a really
complicated graph.

00:16:19.427 --> 00:16:21.510
You might consider using
some frameworks that help

00:16:21.510 --> 00:16:24.230
you do that such as Kubernetes.

00:16:24.230 --> 00:16:25.980
There are some other
tools out there, too.

00:16:25.980 --> 00:16:28.140
And credentials is
another issue as well.

00:16:28.140 --> 00:16:30.060
On your laptop, you just
just happily connect

00:16:30.060 --> 00:16:33.570
to any kind of services that
you are authorized to use.

00:16:33.570 --> 00:16:35.508
But in a container,
it's usually running

00:16:35.508 --> 00:16:36.800
in a different identify, right?

00:16:36.800 --> 00:16:38.550
Remember, that's like
a whole new computer

00:16:38.550 --> 00:16:40.550
that happens to be running
on your host machine.

00:16:40.550 --> 00:16:43.410
It might know nothing whatsoever
about your host machine's

00:16:43.410 --> 00:16:45.930
credentials or
environmental variables,

00:16:45.930 --> 00:16:46.930
and things like that.

00:16:46.930 --> 00:16:50.230
So there are issues
there, as well.

00:16:50.230 --> 00:16:53.410
But we'll probably not
get into that today.

00:16:53.410 --> 00:16:55.500
OK, any questions
up to this point?

