00:18:33.946 --> 00:18:36.730
is there a way to [INAUDIBLE]?

00:18:36.730 --> 00:18:38.020
YU-HAN LIU: Yes, yes.

00:18:38.020 --> 00:18:42.190
So the question was, how do you
do, like, incremental update

00:18:42.190 --> 00:18:43.000
of Docker images?

00:18:43.000 --> 00:18:44.740
Yes, when you build
a Docker image,

00:18:44.740 --> 00:18:46.670
it eventually has this
concept of layers.

00:18:46.670 --> 00:18:48.503
Usually, you can ignore
that because Docker,

00:18:48.503 --> 00:18:50.700
the software, Docker
itself handles that for you

00:18:50.700 --> 00:18:53.170
in saying that it caches, right?

00:18:53.170 --> 00:18:54.910
You are building an
image, which really

00:18:54.910 --> 00:18:57.070
means you start somewhere,
a starting point,

00:18:57.070 --> 00:18:58.780
and then start
adding dependencies

00:18:58.780 --> 00:19:00.990
to it which your project needs.

00:19:00.990 --> 00:19:02.680
And eventually,
adding your code that

00:19:02.680 --> 00:19:05.352
has to be executed on top
of all that dependency,

00:19:05.352 --> 00:19:07.810
you're not really downloading
everything every single time.

00:19:07.810 --> 00:19:09.320
We'll see that in a bit.

00:19:09.320 --> 00:19:12.430
So parts that have
been downloaded once

00:19:12.430 --> 00:19:14.290
will be cached on
your system somewhere,

00:19:14.290 --> 00:19:16.610
and then next time, when
you build on top of that,

00:19:16.610 --> 00:19:20.010
you'll not be
re-downloading again, OK?

00:19:20.010 --> 00:19:22.500
But that's handled
for you automatically.

00:19:22.500 --> 00:19:23.156
Yes?

